{%- comment -%}
  The three times the inbound endpoint refuses to do the obvious thing:
  mail a machine sent, a sender on the blocklist, and a sender in a loop.

  Refusals deserve more test than features. Nobody notices when one stops
  working until the consequence arrives by itself, and one of these
  knowingly loses email.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% liquid
  function _ = 'tests/support/purge'
  function fx = 'tests/fixtures/mail'

  comment
    Mail a machine sent opens a ticket, because somebody wrote in and
    something is happening, and is flagged so the consumer never answers
    it. Answering it is how two auto responders talk all night.
  endcomment
  function auto = 'tests/support/post', from: 'Someone Away <away@tests.invalid>', subject: 'Automatic reply: Lift is stuck between floors', headers: fx.out_of_office, cc: null, text: 'I am away until Monday.', to: null
  function contract = 'modules/tests/assertions/equal', contract: contract, given: auto.new_ticket, expected: true, field_name: 'an out of office still opens a ticket'
  function contract = 'modules/tests/assertions/equal', contract: contract, given: auto.automated, expected: true, field_name: 'and is marked as sent by a machine'
  function contract = 'modules/tests/assertions/equal', contract: contract, given: auto.automated_reason, expected: 'auto-submitted', field_name: 'with the rule that caught it'

  comment
    A blocked sender is refused at the door: nothing written, and a 200
    rather than an error, because the refusal is permanent and a 5xx
    would have the provider deliver the same mail every few hours for
    three days.
  endcomment
  assign block = { "value": "nuisance@tests.invalid", "scope": "email", "reason": "test", "blocked_by": null }
  function blocked = 'modules/support/commands/blocked_senders/create', object: block
  function refused = 'tests/support/post', from: 'A Nuisance <nuisance@tests.invalid>', subject: 'Lift is stuck between floors', headers: fx.opening, cc: null, text: 'Again.', to: null
  function contract = 'modules/tests/assertions/equal', contract: contract, given: refused.blocked, expected: true, field_name: 'a blocked sender is refused'
  function contract = 'modules/tests/assertions/equal', contract: contract, given: refused.status, expected: 200, field_name: 'with a 200, so the provider does not retry for three days'
  function nobody = 'modules/support/queries/contacts/find_by_email', email: 'nuisance@tests.invalid'
  function contract = 'modules/tests/assertions/equal', contract: contract, given: nobody, expected: null, field_name: 'and not even a contact is written'

  comment
    Twenty emails an hour from one sender is a loop, a broken
    integration or an attack, and all three want the same answer. This
    is the one place the module knowingly loses email, so it is the one
    that most deserves a test saying so out loud.
  endcomment
  comment
    Twenty messages are seeded straight onto one ticket rather than
    posted through the door. Posting twenty one real emails took the
    whole run past the twenty second gateway timeout and the suite
    started answering 504 instead of a result.

    What is under test is the guard at the boundary, not the counting of
    its own writes: the limit reads how many inbound messages this
    sender has in the last hour, and it does not care which code path
    wrote them. The twenty first email is a real one, posted through the
    door like any other.

    The first draft of this test posted twenty copies of one Message-ID
    and counted one message, because the duplicate guard caught the other
    nineteen. That is worth knowing if anyone widens this later.
  endcomment
  function first = 'tests/support/post', from: 'A Loop <loop@tests.invalid>', subject: 'Loop', headers: null, cc: null, text: 'Again.', to: null
  function loop_ticket = 'modules/support/queries/tickets/find', id: first.ticket_id, number: null
  function loop_contact = 'modules/support/queries/contacts/find_by_email', email: 'loop@tests.invalid'

  for i in (2..20)
    assign seeded = { "ticket_id": loop_ticket.id, "kind": "inbound", "contact_id": loop_contact.id, "user_id": null, "from_address": "loop@tests.invalid", "to_addresses": null, "cc_addresses": null, "subject": "Loop", "body_html": "", "body_text": "Again.", "quoted_html": "", "message_id_header": null, "in_reply_to": null, "references": null, "provider_message_id": null, "delivery_status": null, "match_reason": "seeded", "raw_headers": null }
    function _ = 'modules/support/commands/messages/create', object: seeded
  endfor

  assign loop_subject = 'Re: Loop [#' | append: loop_ticket.number | append: ']'
  function over = 'tests/support/post', from: 'A Loop <loop@tests.invalid>', subject: loop_subject, headers: null, cc: null, text: 'Again.', to: null
  function contract = 'modules/tests/assertions/equal', contract: contract, given: over.suspected_loop, expected: true, field_name: 'the twenty first email in an hour is dropped'
  function contract = 'modules/tests/assertions/equal', contract: contract, given: over.status, expected: 200, field_name: 'and answered 200, because a retry would bring it straight back'

  function _ = 'tests/support/purge'
%}
